<content>
  <section><title>Activity</title>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>Each expression is written as the product of factors. Write an equivalent expression in standard form.</item>
</list>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
      <item> \((3x)^2\) </item>
    </list>
  <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para>\(9x^2\)</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example><br/>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
      <item> \(7x \cdot 7x\) </item>
    </list>
  <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para> \(49x^2\)</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example><br/>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
      <item> \((x+4)(x+4)\) </item>
    </list>
  <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para> \(x^2+8x+16\)</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example><br/>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
      <item> \((x+1)^2\) </item>
    </list>
  <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para>\(x^2+2x+1\)</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example><br/>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="5" type="enumerated">
      <item> \((x−7)^2\) </item>
    </list>
  <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para>\(x^2−14x+49\)</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example><br/>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="6" type="enumerated">
      <item> \((x+n)^2\) </item>
    </list>
  <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para>\(x^2+2nx+n^2\)</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example><br/>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>Why do you think the following expressions can be described as <term data-schema-version="1.0" data-store="glossary-tooltip">perfect square</term>s?</item>
</list>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para class="os-raise-noindent">\(x^2+6x+9\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;x^2−16x+64\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;x^2+ \frac {1}{3}x+ \frac {1}{36}\)</para>
  <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para>If these expressions are written in factored form, each one is the square of a linear expression:</para>
  <para>\((x+3)^2\), \((x−8)^2\), and \((x+\frac {1}{6})^2\).</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example><br/>
